WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.710
NARRATOR: Welcome to Academy
Dialogues, It Starts With Us.

00:00:02.710 --> 00:00:04.680
A safe space for
honest conversation

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:06.210
and authentic thoughts.

00:00:06.210 --> 00:00:08.610
Our conversation today
is owning your brand.

00:00:08.610 --> 00:00:11.010
Guiding this conversation
is Academy governor,

00:00:11.010 --> 00:00:14.420
award-winning film and TV
producer, best-selling author

00:00:14.420 --> 00:00:15.810
Devon Franklin.

00:00:15.810 --> 00:00:19.050
Joining Devon, producer
Debra Martin Chase.

00:00:19.050 --> 00:00:20.850
Film producer,
digital publisher,

00:00:20.850 --> 00:00:24.210
and creator of the Hollywood
black list, Franklin Leonard.

00:00:24.210 --> 00:00:28.320
President and managing
partner of M88, Phil Sun.

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:29.562
On to you, Devon.

00:00:29.562 --> 00:00:31.270
DEVON FRANKLIN: Hey
hey, what's up y'all?

00:00:31.270 --> 00:00:33.640
Thank you for being here.

00:00:33.640 --> 00:00:36.430
This is another addition
of Academy Dialogues.

00:00:36.430 --> 00:00:38.730
As an Academy
governor, we are so

00:00:38.730 --> 00:00:41.760
excited to be a
part of this series.

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:45.660
It's really about bringing
conversations to the forefront

00:00:45.660 --> 00:00:48.000
that we traditionally
don't have.

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:49.380
It's all about representation.

00:00:49.380 --> 00:00:50.490
It's all about inclusion.

00:00:50.490 --> 00:00:53.940
And one of the ways to get
there is to talk and communicate

00:00:53.940 --> 00:00:55.590
about uncomfortable truths.

00:00:55.590 --> 00:00:57.510
To talk about where
we are and where

00:00:57.510 --> 00:00:59.500
we need to go as an industry.

00:00:59.500 --> 00:01:01.290
So I just want to say what's up.

00:01:01.290 --> 00:01:02.460
Welcome.

00:01:02.460 --> 00:01:05.370
Debra and Phil and
Franklin, what's up man?

00:01:05.370 --> 00:01:07.773
How y'all doing today?

00:01:07.773 --> 00:01:08.690
Debra, you're on mute.

00:01:08.690 --> 00:01:10.025
We've got to unmute.

00:01:10.025 --> 00:01:10.900
We've got to unmute--

00:01:10.900 --> 00:01:12.567
DEBRA MARTIN CHASE:
We've got to unmute.

00:01:12.567 --> 00:01:13.528
Good to see you guys.

00:01:13.528 --> 00:01:14.570
DEVON FRANKLIN: Good to--

00:01:14.570 --> 00:01:15.340
FRANKLIN LEONARD:
We've got to unmute.

00:01:15.340 --> 00:01:17.470
We should just put that
on a t-shirt, right there.

00:01:17.470 --> 00:01:18.678
I feel like that's a mantra--

00:01:18.678 --> 00:01:20.470
DEBRA MARTIN CHASE:
The daily conversation.

00:01:20.470 --> 00:01:22.450
FRANKLIN LEONARD:
--for going forward.

00:01:22.450 --> 00:01:23.260
PHILLIP SUN: Yeah, but some
people have got to mute though.

00:01:23.260 --> 00:01:24.940
Some people have got to mute.

00:01:24.940 --> 00:01:26.050
FRANKLIN LEONARD: That's
a very good point.

00:01:26.050 --> 00:01:28.070
PHILLIP SUN: I'll go on
the other end of that.

00:01:28.070 --> 00:01:29.530
DEBRA MARTIN CHASE: Thank
you Phil, thank you, Phil.

00:01:29.530 --> 00:01:30.260
DEVON FRANKLIN: I love it.

00:01:30.260 --> 00:01:30.760
I love it.

00:01:30.760 --> 00:01:34.120
So even before we get started,
listen Franklin and Phil,

00:01:34.120 --> 00:01:37.810
I just want to just note
that we, all three of us,

00:01:37.810 --> 00:01:40.900
are grateful to share the
airspace with Debra Martin

00:01:40.900 --> 00:01:43.900
Chase on this conversation.

00:01:43.900 --> 00:01:45.890
I want to just note
that she is a legend.

00:01:45.890 --> 00:01:48.820
She is a trailblazer.

00:01:48.820 --> 00:01:51.910
If we could do half of what
she's done in this business,

00:01:51.910 --> 00:01:53.230
we will have done a whole lot.

00:01:53.230 --> 00:01:56.560
So, I'm just honored to
be here with all of you,

00:01:56.560 --> 00:01:58.730
but specifically you Debra.

00:01:58.730 --> 00:01:59.740
So thank you.

00:01:59.740 --> 00:02:00.190
DEBRA MARTIN CHASE: Thank you.

00:02:00.190 --> 00:02:02.565
DEVON FRANKLIN: So I just want
to jump in and really kind

00:02:02.565 --> 00:02:07.270
of dive into what I feel is a
conversation that we have not

00:02:07.270 --> 00:02:09.520
had and we are not having.

00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:11.200
And I really want to
talk about finding

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:14.650
success outside of the
traditional Hollywood system.

00:02:14.650 --> 00:02:18.280
All of us have done
that and are doing that.

00:02:18.280 --> 00:02:21.910
So I really want to get
into the system itself.

00:02:21.910 --> 00:02:23.080
Is the system working?

00:02:23.080 --> 00:02:24.310
Is the system failing?

00:02:24.310 --> 00:02:27.790
What are the changes that
can be made in the system?

00:02:27.790 --> 00:02:30.920
Because we talk about this
institution of Hollywood,

00:02:30.920 --> 00:02:33.940
but very few people
have had the inside look

00:02:33.940 --> 00:02:36.820
into the system of Hollywood
like the four of us.

00:02:36.820 --> 00:02:39.280
So Debra, why don't
we start with you.

00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:40.270
obviously?

00:02:40.270 --> 00:02:44.500
For those who may not know,
you are a Harvard Law graduate.

00:02:44.500 --> 00:02:45.950
You are an attorney.

00:02:45.950 --> 00:02:47.800
But you also took a
turn in your career,

00:02:47.800 --> 00:02:51.040
where you became an intern
for the legendary Frank Price,

00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:52.630
chairman of Columbia Pictures.

00:02:52.630 --> 00:02:55.540
And so, you have an intimate
knowledge of the studio system,

00:02:55.540 --> 00:03:00.040
but you chose to blaze trails as
a producer, a content creator.

00:03:00.040 --> 00:03:03.430
So talk about your
experience inside the system

00:03:03.430 --> 00:03:06.010
and what led you to
ultimately make the decision

00:03:06.010 --> 00:03:08.590
to work outside of it.

00:03:08.590 --> 00:03:10.070
DEBRA MARTIN CHASE: Two things.

00:03:10.070 --> 00:03:13.870
One was that, having had
a career as an attorney,

00:03:13.870 --> 00:03:19.660
that I put on the shelf to make
movies because I loved movies

00:03:19.660 --> 00:03:22.600
and it was a passion and
it was a dream to do it.

00:03:22.600 --> 00:03:24.850
Getting the glimpse
into the system

00:03:24.850 --> 00:03:28.090
while I was working with Frank,
and he he's my first mentor.

00:03:28.090 --> 00:03:31.750
He was so good to me and
gave me the opportunity

00:03:31.750 --> 00:03:32.950
as his executive assistant.

00:03:32.950 --> 00:03:36.130
It usually goes to the
white guy, frankly.

00:03:36.130 --> 00:03:37.570
I went with him to his meetings.

00:03:37.570 --> 00:03:39.670
I sat with him in the
office and asked questions.

00:03:39.670 --> 00:03:42.880
And so I really got a
look at what was going on.

00:03:42.880 --> 00:03:45.280
And I just found, at
least at that time,

00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:49.390
the politics were so heavy.

00:03:49.390 --> 00:03:51.920
It wasn't about-- he
was about filmmaking,

00:03:51.920 --> 00:03:54.370
but in terms of the rank
and file, the executives,

00:03:54.370 --> 00:03:58.180
it was really about positioning
and posturing and fear

00:03:58.180 --> 00:04:00.050
and parking spaces.

00:04:00.050 --> 00:04:04.350
And it really wasn't
about the work.

00:04:04.350 --> 00:04:08.350
But secondly,
particularly once he left,

00:04:08.350 --> 00:04:11.740
and he was kind of ousted
in a very big coup,

00:04:11.740 --> 00:04:13.720
there was no place for me.

00:04:13.720 --> 00:04:15.700
Like nobody wanted--

00:04:15.700 --> 00:04:18.160
I used to say, I felt
like the lone Democrat

00:04:18.160 --> 00:04:20.170
in the Republican White House.

00:04:20.170 --> 00:04:24.130
As a black woman, nobody really
was interested in the things

00:04:24.130 --> 00:04:27.130
that I was interested in doing.

00:04:27.130 --> 00:04:32.140
And as will be a theme, I think,
throughout this conversation,

00:04:32.140 --> 00:04:35.710
there is diversity, and
then there's inclusion.

00:04:35.710 --> 00:04:37.690
And I was not being included.

00:04:41.040 --> 00:04:44.440
They weren't inviting me
to the dinner parties.

00:04:44.440 --> 00:04:48.070
They weren't building
relationships with me.

00:04:48.070 --> 00:04:52.120
And I'm a very,
very social person.

00:04:52.120 --> 00:04:56.380
I have friends all over the
world, from all walks of life,

00:04:56.380 --> 00:04:57.680
colors, whatever.

00:04:57.680 --> 00:04:59.270
And it just wasn't
a welcoming place.

00:04:59.270 --> 00:05:01.228
And I'm like, I'm not
living my life like this.

00:05:01.228 --> 00:05:03.250
I mean, I want to make
movies, but I also

00:05:03.250 --> 00:05:06.350
want to be appreciated
for who I am.

00:05:06.350 --> 00:05:08.002
DEVON FRANKLIN: Yeah, wow.

00:05:08.002 --> 00:05:10.210
We're going to unpack the
diversity versus inclusion,

00:05:10.210 --> 00:05:12.280
because you are 1,000% right.

00:05:12.280 --> 00:05:14.500
And I think that's
maybe one of the reasons

00:05:14.500 --> 00:05:17.860
why, as people of
color, there has always

00:05:17.860 --> 00:05:19.840
been a challenge within
the Hollywood system.

00:05:19.840 --> 00:05:21.970
Because of that word
diversity, which

00:05:21.970 --> 00:05:26.650
is so different than the
idea behind inclusion.

00:05:26.650 --> 00:05:28.840
So Franklin, I want
to toss to you.

00:05:28.840 --> 00:05:30.790
Debra mentions politics.

00:05:30.790 --> 00:05:33.970
You were a studio
executive for Universal.

00:05:33.970 --> 00:05:36.400
Talk about the politics.

00:05:36.400 --> 00:05:40.150
Talk about the studio system
and what you saw there,

00:05:40.150 --> 00:05:43.450
or what your experience was
that made you feel, or made

00:05:43.450 --> 00:05:44.860
you decide, you know what?

00:05:44.860 --> 00:05:46.510
This is maybe not
the life for me,

00:05:46.510 --> 00:05:48.700
and there's something different.

00:05:48.700 --> 00:05:51.480
FRANKLIN LEONARD: Yeah I
think like Debra mentioned,

00:05:51.480 --> 00:05:54.630
I came into the industry
I think somewhat naive.

00:05:54.630 --> 00:05:55.810
I wanted to make movies.

00:05:55.810 --> 00:05:57.060
I wanted to make great movies.

00:05:57.060 --> 00:05:58.920
And I wanted those
movies to make money.

00:05:58.920 --> 00:06:00.360
And those were my
priorities as I

00:06:00.360 --> 00:06:02.400
thought through the
material that came in.

00:06:02.400 --> 00:06:03.787
I would look at the culture.

00:06:03.787 --> 00:06:05.370
I would look at
audiences, and I would

00:06:05.370 --> 00:06:08.970
try to identify material
that might work with those.

00:06:08.970 --> 00:06:13.530
And whenever I would bring
material the conversation,

00:06:13.530 --> 00:06:15.840
or perspective to
the conversation,

00:06:15.840 --> 00:06:17.400
I realized that the
responses weren't

00:06:17.400 --> 00:06:20.190
driven by of rational
analysis of the argument

00:06:20.190 --> 00:06:21.510
that I was trying to make.

00:06:21.510 --> 00:06:24.420
It was driven by, OK how does
this response position me

00:06:24.420 --> 00:06:27.210
vis-a-vis other people at the
company that will allow me

00:06:27.210 --> 00:06:30.030
to ascend to certain statures?

00:06:30.030 --> 00:06:32.830
And I understand the
impulse behind that.

00:06:32.830 --> 00:06:33.330
Right?

00:06:33.330 --> 00:06:34.440
People have mortgages.

00:06:34.440 --> 00:06:36.120
They have kids in
private school.

00:06:36.120 --> 00:06:37.830
They have their own
career aspirations.

00:06:37.830 --> 00:06:39.450
And they're trying
to secure the bag.

00:06:39.450 --> 00:06:41.850
And that leads to certain
incentive structures

00:06:41.850 --> 00:06:43.890
that lead to certain behavior.

00:06:43.890 --> 00:06:45.830
I've just never
been good at that.

00:06:45.830 --> 00:06:49.350
And I recognize it as something
that is, in some ways,

00:06:49.350 --> 00:06:51.300
a professional failure of mine.

00:06:51.300 --> 00:06:54.690
I'm just not good at navigating
corporate bureaucracy.

00:06:54.690 --> 00:06:58.560
I am good at advocacy, and
I am good at identifying

00:06:58.560 --> 00:07:03.050
good material and
getting it to the public.

00:07:03.050 --> 00:07:05.390
And so, I realized very
early on that I was never

00:07:05.390 --> 00:07:07.140
going to thrive in an
environment that was

00:07:07.140 --> 00:07:09.343
driven by that system of rules.

00:07:09.343 --> 00:07:11.010
And it became very
clear to me that if I

00:07:11.010 --> 00:07:13.740
was going to be successful
in this industry,

00:07:13.740 --> 00:07:16.020
or likely any other
frankly, I would have

00:07:16.020 --> 00:07:18.360
to try to do it my own way.

00:07:18.360 --> 00:07:23.340
And I left Universal to go to
Overbrook to work for Will.

00:07:23.340 --> 00:07:25.880
Part of that exit was
them committing to me

00:07:25.880 --> 00:07:28.380
that I could develop the black
list as a for-profit venture,

00:07:28.380 --> 00:07:30.180
simultaneous to working there.

00:07:30.180 --> 00:07:32.850
So I was working 9:00
AM to 9:00 PM for Will,

00:07:32.850 --> 00:07:35.970
and then 9:00 PM to
2:00 AM for myself.

00:07:35.970 --> 00:07:38.280
And two years later, I was
able to launch the company.

00:07:38.280 --> 00:07:42.180
Literally almost
eight years ago today.

00:07:42.180 --> 00:07:45.750
And I had that runway
because of the understanding

00:07:45.750 --> 00:07:49.230
that I think Will and James
Lassiter had too, which is,

00:07:49.230 --> 00:07:51.280
you can't do it alone.

00:07:51.280 --> 00:07:55.115
And you need somebody
to say, OK look,

00:07:55.115 --> 00:07:56.740
I don't know how this
is going to work.

00:07:56.740 --> 00:07:59.010
But as long as you
do right by us,

00:07:59.010 --> 00:08:01.403
we're going to make it possible
to you do right by you.

00:08:01.403 --> 00:08:02.820
And everybody is
going to be good.

00:08:02.820 --> 00:08:05.100
And I'm infinitely
thankful to them for that,

00:08:05.100 --> 00:08:08.490
because they really
did set me on my way,

00:08:08.490 --> 00:08:10.630
and here we are today.

00:08:10.630 --> 00:08:13.490
DEVON FRANKLIN: Yeah, which is
great because, to that point,

00:08:13.490 --> 00:08:17.330
having that type of
support and incubation

00:08:17.330 --> 00:08:21.120
is a critical element
of, I believe,

00:08:21.120 --> 00:08:23.012
anyone in this business
to be successful

00:08:23.012 --> 00:08:23.970
has to have incubation.

00:08:23.970 --> 00:08:26.640
Because this is an
apprenticeship business.

00:08:26.640 --> 00:08:28.530
It's a very difficult
business just

00:08:28.530 --> 00:08:31.980
to come into, without having
learned every aspect of it,

00:08:31.980 --> 00:08:34.320
and having others who have
already learned it help us

00:08:34.320 --> 00:08:35.580
along the way.

00:08:35.580 --> 00:08:39.929
So listen, I share the same
level of great gratitude

00:08:39.929 --> 00:08:41.039
for Will and James.

00:08:41.039 --> 00:08:43.539
I got my start as an intern
for them, and all of that.

00:08:43.539 --> 00:08:45.240
So without that
type of incubation,

00:08:45.240 --> 00:08:46.860
certainly wouldn't
be here today.

00:08:46.860 --> 00:08:51.150
So Phil, I want to talk and
just talk about this idea of you

00:08:51.150 --> 00:08:54.510
just recently exited the system.

00:08:54.510 --> 00:08:55.800
You were an agent.

00:08:55.800 --> 00:08:58.980
One of the top agents for WME.

00:08:58.980 --> 00:09:02.940
Representing some of the most
dynamic and successful talent

00:09:02.940 --> 00:09:03.900
in the world.

00:09:03.900 --> 00:09:06.390
But recently, the
news of your exit

00:09:06.390 --> 00:09:12.750
to start the management division
for Macro shook the industry.

00:09:12.750 --> 00:09:14.130
I personally was like, what?

00:09:14.130 --> 00:09:15.990
Phil Sun is gone?

00:09:15.990 --> 00:09:17.930
What is going on
with these places?

00:09:17.930 --> 00:09:18.930
I'll be honest with you.

00:09:18.930 --> 00:09:23.970
So, can we talk about what
was going on in the system?

00:09:23.970 --> 00:09:25.770
What were the politics
or the challenges

00:09:25.770 --> 00:09:28.440
that you may have
faced, that ultimately

00:09:28.440 --> 00:09:33.060
may have led to making the
decision to go out on your own

00:09:33.060 --> 00:09:35.860
and start this new
incredible endeavor?

00:09:35.860 --> 00:09:39.000
PHILLIP SUN: Yeah,
it's been a journey

00:09:39.000 --> 00:09:41.670
to where we got to with M88.

00:09:41.670 --> 00:09:44.850
And it's certainly,
to your point

00:09:44.850 --> 00:09:48.840
and also to Franklin's point,
my new venture is not alone.

00:09:48.840 --> 00:09:51.810
Like Charles King, who
I partner with at Macro,

00:09:51.810 --> 00:09:57.090
who has really built a pathway
for many people of color

00:09:57.090 --> 00:09:58.572
outside of the system.

00:09:58.572 --> 00:10:00.030
We stand on his
shoulders for sure,

00:10:00.030 --> 00:10:01.680
as we build this new company.

00:10:01.680 --> 00:10:06.780
But the system itself
and representation, it's

00:10:06.780 --> 00:10:07.470
tricky, right?

00:10:07.470 --> 00:10:12.810
Because in representation,
you need to learn the system.

00:10:12.810 --> 00:10:15.120
In order to advocate
for stories of people

00:10:15.120 --> 00:10:17.520
of color and advocate
for your clients fully,

00:10:17.520 --> 00:10:19.590
you need to understand,
what is the strength

00:10:19.590 --> 00:10:21.880
and how they have
done it in the past.

00:10:21.880 --> 00:10:26.130
So my journey at the agency
was actually, for the 15 years

00:10:26.130 --> 00:10:28.950
I was there from mailroom
to senior partner,

00:10:28.950 --> 00:10:33.500
it was all a big
educational system for me.

00:10:33.500 --> 00:10:34.710
Right?

00:10:34.710 --> 00:10:37.170
And like you said,
you needed mentors

00:10:37.170 --> 00:10:39.540
to kind of excel in this
business, and any sort

00:10:39.540 --> 00:10:42.810
of system, and unfortunately--

00:10:42.810 --> 00:10:44.700
I had a few great mentors,
Charles King being

00:10:44.700 --> 00:10:48.140
one of them, Patrick Weitzel.

00:10:48.140 --> 00:10:51.050
But you need that
continued growth

00:10:51.050 --> 00:10:52.800
in order to continue to grow.

00:10:52.800 --> 00:10:53.300
Right?

00:10:53.300 --> 00:10:58.010
So the representation
system, if we're

00:10:58.010 --> 00:11:01.550
talking for real, the
agencies and the old school

00:11:01.550 --> 00:11:04.130
representation
systems, are built

00:11:04.130 --> 00:11:07.560
to represent a certain
type of talent.

00:11:07.560 --> 00:11:08.060
Right?

00:11:08.060 --> 00:11:14.000
And it's built to operate in an
older system across the board

00:11:14.000 --> 00:11:15.740
with studios,
producers, et cetera.

00:11:15.740 --> 00:11:16.330
Right?

00:11:16.330 --> 00:11:18.645
Like to what Debra
was talking about,

00:11:18.645 --> 00:11:20.270
you can do business
people, but there's

00:11:20.270 --> 00:11:22.645
a different thing where you're
going to dinners with them

00:11:22.645 --> 00:11:26.200
and going on rafting trips and
having friendships with them.

00:11:26.200 --> 00:11:27.650
Right?

00:11:27.650 --> 00:11:30.950
I think the representation
game is evolving,

00:11:30.950 --> 00:11:33.950
because now the clientele that
is being represented and also

00:11:33.950 --> 00:11:37.370
making money at the box office
and putting butts in seats

00:11:37.370 --> 00:11:39.890
and eyeballs on
television screens,

00:11:39.890 --> 00:11:41.630
they don't look like
they did before.

00:11:41.630 --> 00:11:43.220
They're multicultural.

00:11:43.220 --> 00:11:46.460
They're the new global majority.

00:11:46.460 --> 00:11:49.490
So now the system
is doing its best

00:11:49.490 --> 00:11:53.180
to try to catch up to
having representatives

00:11:53.180 --> 00:11:57.290
that also look like the
talent that is on screen.

00:11:57.290 --> 00:12:01.160
And we're playing a
little bit from behind is,

00:12:01.160 --> 00:12:03.590
that's not something you
can react to and fix.

00:12:03.590 --> 00:12:05.120
That's a pipeline.

00:12:05.120 --> 00:12:09.740
That's actually putting
efforts in years

00:12:09.740 --> 00:12:14.150
prior to, like it took me 15
years to get to where I am.

00:12:14.150 --> 00:12:16.880
And that's me, I'm unique.

00:12:16.880 --> 00:12:21.950
And now I look down and see
the new future of the agency

00:12:21.950 --> 00:12:24.080
where I left, it
gives me great joy

00:12:24.080 --> 00:12:28.430
to see people who look like
me, look like the world looks.

00:12:28.430 --> 00:12:31.280
But there's such
a long way to go.

00:12:31.280 --> 00:12:35.810
And because the system is built
in a certain way, I felt that--

00:12:35.810 --> 00:12:38.090
and also as you
know agency wars,

00:12:38.090 --> 00:12:40.430
if you do something good
for diversity and inclusion

00:12:40.430 --> 00:12:43.610
at one agency, you
hope it would spread.

00:12:43.610 --> 00:12:46.430
But it can be taken as,
oh this is a WME thing,

00:12:46.430 --> 00:12:48.740
or oh this is a CAA thing,
whether it's amplify,

00:12:48.740 --> 00:12:49.623
whether it's power.

00:12:49.623 --> 00:12:50.540
DEVON FRANKLIN: Right.

00:12:50.540 --> 00:12:56.240
PHILLIP SUN: We kind of put a
ceiling on ourselves, as far as

00:12:56.240 --> 00:12:58.580
how we can move.

00:12:58.580 --> 00:13:00.410
And so all of that
to be said, it

00:13:00.410 --> 00:13:03.320
led me to a decision
of partnering

00:13:03.320 --> 00:13:06.200
with Charles King, who is
a longtime mentor of mine,

00:13:06.200 --> 00:13:09.410
with the support of my agency.

00:13:09.410 --> 00:13:11.960
And becoming a third party.

00:13:11.960 --> 00:13:13.910
Becoming a manager.

00:13:13.910 --> 00:13:16.100
Where we can push the envelope.

00:13:16.100 --> 00:13:17.810
We're building a
representation firm now

00:13:17.810 --> 00:13:20.990
that not only represents
the global majority,

00:13:20.990 --> 00:13:23.210
but is built within from
the global majority.

00:13:23.210 --> 00:13:27.430
The point of view of
diversity at agencies

00:13:27.430 --> 00:13:29.990
is usually an afterthought,
it's a program.

00:13:29.990 --> 00:13:33.415
It's a reaction to the times of
what's going on in the world.

00:13:33.415 --> 00:13:34.790
This representation
firm is built

00:13:34.790 --> 00:13:38.011
from the core of those
beliefs from the onset.

00:13:38.011 --> 00:13:38.803
DEVON FRANKLIN: Mm.

00:13:38.803 --> 00:13:40.930
PHILLIP SUN: There
is no afterthought.

00:13:40.930 --> 00:13:41.930
There is no reaction.

00:13:41.930 --> 00:13:43.640
It is just us.

00:13:43.640 --> 00:13:47.600
And in a world that's chasing
culture, I think all of us

00:13:47.600 --> 00:13:50.630
have seen the studios
and the buyers

00:13:50.630 --> 00:13:55.340
chasing what is a culture,
who is a culture mover?

00:13:55.340 --> 00:14:01.310
We're trying to bring culture
within one institution,

00:14:01.310 --> 00:14:03.620
instead of having
everyone spread out.

00:14:03.620 --> 00:14:07.540
We now have a home, if you
choose and want to come,

00:14:07.540 --> 00:14:09.410
it's here for you.

00:14:09.410 --> 00:14:13.710
And so it it's a complicated
world, the representation

00:14:13.710 --> 00:14:14.210
world.

00:14:14.210 --> 00:14:15.980
Because I think it's the least--

00:14:15.980 --> 00:14:20.540
it's the silo of the industry
that's least spoken about.

00:14:20.540 --> 00:14:23.960
Because there's always
a negative connotation

00:14:23.960 --> 00:14:32.090
to reps, to suits, to whatever
label you want to put on it.

00:14:32.090 --> 00:14:35.390
It's not often positive.

00:14:35.390 --> 00:14:37.550
And we as a community
need to address

00:14:37.550 --> 00:14:40.760
that as well, because
quite frankly,

00:14:40.760 --> 00:14:42.830
the agencies, big
ones especially,

00:14:42.830 --> 00:14:45.410
they're perfect
marketing machines.

00:14:45.410 --> 00:14:50.510
They understand how
to make someone great.

00:14:50.510 --> 00:14:56.150
But we have to understand how
to utilize that in our way,

00:14:56.150 --> 00:15:01.820
how to work with them and push
them to market in our way.

00:15:01.820 --> 00:15:05.000
And hopefully that's what
M88 will help specifically

00:15:05.000 --> 00:15:06.573
the big agencies do.

00:15:06.573 --> 00:15:08.990
DEVON FRANKLIN: Right, and for
those that are watching M88

00:15:08.990 --> 00:15:11.450
is the new company,
the joint venture,

00:15:11.450 --> 00:15:15.320
that Phil Sun has started
with Charles King and Macro.

00:15:15.320 --> 00:15:18.530
I want to ask a potentially
provocative question

00:15:18.530 --> 00:15:21.210
just to all of you.

00:15:21.210 --> 00:15:23.460
Do they not like us?

00:15:23.460 --> 00:15:25.110
When you talk about
all of us have--

00:15:25.110 --> 00:15:27.960
I was, as you all may
know, I was an executive

00:15:27.960 --> 00:15:30.120
at Columbia Pictures
for 10 years,

00:15:30.120 --> 00:15:32.688
and worked my way up from
director of development

00:15:32.688 --> 00:15:34.230
all the way to senior
vise president.

00:15:34.230 --> 00:15:37.980
But going back to
Debra, there weren't

00:15:37.980 --> 00:15:41.010
invitations to hang out
and do the social things

00:15:41.010 --> 00:15:44.250
or be a part of the
social crowd, so to speak.

00:15:44.250 --> 00:15:46.630
I was just working within
the system and doing my best.

00:15:46.630 --> 00:15:48.210
But I just want to
ask that question.

00:15:48.210 --> 00:15:49.730
Is it that they
just don't like us?

00:15:49.730 --> 00:15:52.050
Is the system racist?

00:15:52.050 --> 00:15:54.840
Is the system prejudiced?

00:15:54.840 --> 00:15:56.970
Let's just ask the
honest question.

00:15:56.970 --> 00:15:59.190
What is it?

00:15:59.190 --> 00:16:02.040
So whoever wants to
just jump in on that.

00:16:02.040 --> 00:16:03.870
DEBRA MARTIN CHASE:
I think it's twofold.

00:16:03.870 --> 00:16:04.980
DEVON FRANKLIN: OK.

00:16:04.980 --> 00:16:07.770
DEBRA MARTIN CHASE:
I think, one,

00:16:07.770 --> 00:16:10.410
this is something that people
really don't talk about.

00:16:10.410 --> 00:16:15.120
LA is one of the most racially
segregated cities in America.

00:16:18.330 --> 00:16:21.780
The west side is
kind of all white.

00:16:21.780 --> 00:16:27.210
The east side of town
is more integrated.

00:16:27.210 --> 00:16:30.660
So as a practical
matter, and you know--

00:16:30.660 --> 00:16:34.110
because I also live in
New York, pre-COVID,

00:16:34.110 --> 00:16:35.910
everybody took the subway.

00:16:35.910 --> 00:16:37.290
Everybody walked
down the street.

00:16:37.290 --> 00:16:40.110
So just on a daily
basis, you would

00:16:40.110 --> 00:16:44.010
interact with people that
didn't look like you.

00:16:44.010 --> 00:16:46.710
And it's just a
different makeup.

00:16:46.710 --> 00:16:51.030
So you have people that,
just in their real lives,

00:16:51.030 --> 00:16:53.640
don't know any people of color.

00:16:53.640 --> 00:16:57.420
Except for perhaps,
honestly, the nanny

00:16:57.420 --> 00:17:03.150
or the gardener or somebody that
they do not consider an equal.

00:17:03.150 --> 00:17:06.869
So there's not a comfort level.

00:17:06.869 --> 00:17:09.510
There's this kind of
natural, OK, you're black,

00:17:09.510 --> 00:17:12.150
I'm white, with people.

00:17:12.150 --> 00:17:15.380
So, I think that that's one.

00:17:15.380 --> 00:17:17.960
And then secondly, it
isn't until recently,

00:17:17.960 --> 00:17:21.530
and we can all chart
how it happened,

00:17:21.530 --> 00:17:24.560
that Hollywood finally
recognized that diversity

00:17:24.560 --> 00:17:25.880
is good business.

00:17:25.880 --> 00:17:30.740
For years, we were
saying, guys the world

00:17:30.740 --> 00:17:33.740
does not look like the
west side of Los Angeles.

00:17:33.740 --> 00:17:35.910
And they were still
saying, you know what,

00:17:35.910 --> 00:17:40.783
but I don't know, a black
lead in an hour-long drama.

00:17:40.783 --> 00:17:42.950
I don't know that white
people are going to tune in.

00:17:42.950 --> 00:17:44.780
Because they weren't
talking to people

00:17:44.780 --> 00:17:46.790
that didn't think like they did.

00:17:46.790 --> 00:17:51.230
So, I think once Hollywood
realized-- so therefore,

00:17:51.230 --> 00:17:55.100
there was no incentive to
reach out, to change lifestyle,

00:17:55.100 --> 00:18:00.140
to expand your comfort
level, vision, whatever.

00:18:00.140 --> 00:18:02.120
So Phil you were
kind of alluding

00:18:02.120 --> 00:18:06.650
to this, once Hollywood
said, oh this is money.

00:18:06.650 --> 00:18:09.710
These stars are money.

00:18:09.710 --> 00:18:13.650
The Rock is the biggest star,
one of the biggest stars

00:18:13.650 --> 00:18:14.780
is The Rock.

00:18:14.780 --> 00:18:15.890
Vin Diesel.

00:18:15.890 --> 00:18:20.570
You start going down the list
of sure enough moneymakers now,

00:18:20.570 --> 00:18:22.580
most of them are--

00:18:22.580 --> 00:18:24.800
So it changed.

00:18:24.800 --> 00:18:27.170
It's like, OK well we
need to know people.

00:18:27.170 --> 00:18:31.560
We need to begin to be
familiar with these people.

00:18:31.560 --> 00:18:35.520
They need to like us,
because we need them.

00:18:35.520 --> 00:18:39.290
And so it started to open
things up, but I still find--

00:18:43.488 --> 00:18:45.530
because again, I spend a
lot of time in New York,

00:18:45.530 --> 00:18:48.200
and I'm on the board of
the New York City Ballet,

00:18:48.200 --> 00:18:50.470
and Second Stage Theater
and blah, blah, blah.

00:18:50.470 --> 00:18:52.100
You know, when I walk
into a room there

00:18:52.100 --> 00:18:54.260
and people don't know
me, they're like,

00:18:54.260 --> 00:18:56.600
oh she's in the room,
she must be interesting.

00:18:56.600 --> 00:18:58.610
Let me make a point
to get to know her.

00:18:58.610 --> 00:19:01.460
Here, you go in a
room and they see

00:19:01.460 --> 00:19:05.480
a black woman that's not
Beyonce or Halle or somebody

00:19:05.480 --> 00:19:09.710
that people think,
oh, is important.

00:19:09.710 --> 00:19:11.420
They assume you're
not important.

00:19:11.420 --> 00:19:13.430
They assume they don't
need to know you.

00:19:13.430 --> 00:19:16.790
And I can't tell you how many
times I've been in a room

00:19:16.790 --> 00:19:18.740
and somebody will
overhear my name,

00:19:18.740 --> 00:19:22.660
because we do so much on the
phone pre-COVID, presume.

00:19:22.660 --> 00:19:24.490
And people say, oh my
god Debra, it's you.

00:19:24.490 --> 00:19:25.850
I didn't recognize you.

00:19:25.850 --> 00:19:28.907
And it's a whole
different attitude.

00:19:28.907 --> 00:19:29.740
DEVON FRANKLIN: Wow.

00:19:29.740 --> 00:19:31.302
DEBRA MARTIN CHASE: So, yeah.

00:19:31.302 --> 00:19:32.260
DEVON FRANKLIN: Got it.

00:19:32.260 --> 00:19:33.677
FRANKLIN LEONARD:
I would actually

00:19:33.677 --> 00:19:35.890
challenge the idea that
people have figured out

00:19:35.890 --> 00:19:37.943
that it's good business.

00:19:37.943 --> 00:19:39.110
I think we're getting there.

00:19:39.110 --> 00:19:40.540
I think they're starting
to realize that,

00:19:40.540 --> 00:19:42.430
but I don't think
that they still think

00:19:42.430 --> 00:19:48.460
of black actors, women,
black women, Latinx actors,

00:19:48.460 --> 00:19:51.040
Asian actors, as
being financially

00:19:51.040 --> 00:19:55.870
equivalent to their white
peers in pure economic terms.

00:19:55.870 --> 00:19:57.680
And I say that for two reasons.

00:19:57.680 --> 00:20:01.600
One, the economic argument
has always been there.

00:20:01.600 --> 00:20:04.150
Eddie Murphy was
big in the '80s.

00:20:04.150 --> 00:20:06.610
Denzel Washington
was big in the '90's.

00:20:06.610 --> 00:20:10.580
Will Smith was big in the 2000s.

00:20:10.580 --> 00:20:14.890
We have always been amongst the
biggest movie stars on earth,

00:20:14.890 --> 00:20:17.620
even internationally.

00:20:17.620 --> 00:20:21.058
And I remember going into
studio meetings and saying, hey,

00:20:21.058 --> 00:20:22.600
why don't you make
more black movies?

00:20:22.600 --> 00:20:24.940
And the answer was
always, well we'd love to,

00:20:24.940 --> 00:20:26.620
but we can't sell those abroad.

00:20:26.620 --> 00:20:29.630
People don't want to see
in black faces on screen.

00:20:29.630 --> 00:20:34.250
And here's the thing, that
was and continues to be a lie.

00:20:34.250 --> 00:20:38.110
That requires that you believe
that for all of the last 40

00:20:38.110 --> 00:20:41.980
years, while hip hop was the
most powerful cultural force

00:20:41.980 --> 00:20:43.600
around the world,
and people were

00:20:43.600 --> 00:20:45.940
going to concerts
the world over,

00:20:45.940 --> 00:20:48.580
while people were cheering
for black athletes,

00:20:48.580 --> 00:20:51.070
while people were paying
to see Coming to America

00:20:51.070 --> 00:20:56.440
in Germany in the mid 1980s,
somehow other black actors

00:20:56.440 --> 00:20:57.250
weren't viable.

00:20:57.250 --> 00:20:59.047
Which was not true, period.

00:20:59.047 --> 00:21:00.880
And the numbers have
been run by Stacy Smith

00:21:00.880 --> 00:21:04.180
at USC, that have proven
statistically that there

00:21:04.180 --> 00:21:06.490
is no difference in
the international box

00:21:06.490 --> 00:21:09.970
office of diverse content when
it is supported by marketing

00:21:09.970 --> 00:21:12.220
dollars that the studios
traditionally have not spent.

00:21:12.220 --> 00:21:13.303
PHILLIP SUN: That's right.

00:21:13.303 --> 00:21:15.230
FRANKLIN LEONARD: And I
would say beyond that,

00:21:15.230 --> 00:21:17.800
there is also a
gross undervaluation

00:21:17.800 --> 00:21:20.800
of people not in
front of the camera.

00:21:20.800 --> 00:21:24.310
When a young black woman
walks into an agent's office

00:21:24.310 --> 00:21:27.790
and says, I'm a director, they
don't think to themselves,

00:21:27.790 --> 00:21:29.860
I'm talking to the
next Steven Spielberg.

00:21:29.860 --> 00:21:32.920
They don't think, I'm talking
to the next James Cameron.

00:21:32.920 --> 00:21:35.500
And as a consequence of
that, when Ava DuVernay

00:21:35.500 --> 00:21:38.330
wins the Best Director
Award at Sundance,

00:21:38.330 --> 00:21:41.620
she gets no studio meetings
off the back of that.

00:21:41.620 --> 00:21:42.160
None.

00:21:42.160 --> 00:21:42.790
Not one.

00:21:42.790 --> 00:21:45.160
And you can ask her
about that right.

00:21:45.160 --> 00:21:47.882
Whereas, if you are
Colin Trevorrow,

00:21:47.882 --> 00:21:49.840
and you win the Best
Director Award at Sundance

00:21:49.840 --> 00:21:51.590
for Safety Not Guaranteed,
your next movie

00:21:51.590 --> 00:21:55.300
is a Jurassic Park sequel.

00:21:55.300 --> 00:21:58.960
And again, it comes from
a gross undervaluation

00:21:58.960 --> 00:22:02.120
of people of color, of women,
of all of these things.

00:22:02.120 --> 00:22:04.750
And the reality is we need to
put a very fine point on it.

00:22:04.750 --> 00:22:05.980
That is racism.

00:22:05.980 --> 00:22:06.970
That is sexism.

00:22:06.970 --> 00:22:08.708
And it's perpetuated
by a system that

00:22:08.708 --> 00:22:10.750
allows people to think
that they're justifying it

00:22:10.750 --> 00:22:13.750
because they're saying, oh well,
if you look at the numbers,

00:22:13.750 --> 00:22:15.730
these people have less value.

00:22:15.730 --> 00:22:18.400
I challenge anybody
who makes that claim.

00:22:18.400 --> 00:22:19.390
Show me the numbers.

00:22:19.390 --> 00:22:20.560
Give me your receipts.

00:22:20.560 --> 00:22:21.880
I'll become your best advocate.

00:22:21.880 --> 00:22:23.660
I've been saying
this for 15 years,

00:22:23.660 --> 00:22:25.330
and no one has
presented those numbers.

00:22:25.330 --> 00:22:26.140
DEBRA MARTIN CHASE: But
let me say one thing,

00:22:26.140 --> 00:22:27.820
and Phil I know you
want to jump in.

00:22:27.820 --> 00:22:28.903
PHILLIP SUN: No, go ahead.

00:22:28.903 --> 00:22:31.750
DEBRA MARTIN CHASE: What
happened with Eddie, Denzel,

00:22:31.750 --> 00:22:34.810
and Will, people said,
well they're white.

00:22:34.810 --> 00:22:38.560
I mean they're black, but people
don't think of them as black.

00:22:38.560 --> 00:22:41.977
So they took them
out of the equation.

00:22:41.977 --> 00:22:43.060
FRANKLIN LEONARD: Exactly.

00:22:43.060 --> 00:22:44.143
DEBRA MARTIN CHASE: Right?

00:22:44.143 --> 00:22:46.690
Which that's what racism does.

00:22:46.690 --> 00:22:49.650
It's like you can't see
the truth for the truth.

00:22:49.650 --> 00:22:51.358
PHILLIP SUN: Well when
you can't beat it,

00:22:51.358 --> 00:22:53.350
you try to embrace it, right?

00:22:53.350 --> 00:22:57.670
Also, here's what ultimately
this all comes down to.

00:22:57.670 --> 00:22:59.980
Your question was, in
my personal opinion,

00:22:59.980 --> 00:23:02.320
I don't think the industry
is inherently racist.

00:23:02.320 --> 00:23:04.660
I think it's, as I've said
before, older systems.

00:23:04.660 --> 00:23:06.340
But here's the deal.

00:23:06.340 --> 00:23:09.123
All these decisions,
all of these movies

00:23:09.123 --> 00:23:10.540
that are being
made, content being

00:23:10.540 --> 00:23:12.700
made, people being elevated.

00:23:12.700 --> 00:23:14.890
It all comes down to who
is present in the smallest

00:23:14.890 --> 00:23:17.360
of rooms of decision makers.

00:23:17.360 --> 00:23:21.340
Until that changes,
you're lacking

00:23:21.340 --> 00:23:24.760
a point of view, when the
smallest room is created, when

00:23:24.760 --> 00:23:27.640
the decisions are made
to fight for the things

00:23:27.640 --> 00:23:31.150
that we all as a
collective already know.

00:23:31.150 --> 00:23:34.060
Is that when Moonlight
is successful.

00:23:34.060 --> 00:23:35.650
When Black Panther
is successful.

00:23:35.650 --> 00:23:37.870
When Crazy Rich
Asians is successful.

00:23:37.870 --> 00:23:41.870
When Tigertail
gets rave reviews.

00:23:41.870 --> 00:23:43.560
It's not a surprise.

00:23:43.560 --> 00:23:44.590
It works.

00:23:44.590 --> 00:23:47.740
It has worked over and over,
but because it's so spaced out,

00:23:47.740 --> 00:23:51.010
because there are still
barriers to entry,

00:23:51.010 --> 00:23:54.310
everything seems
like an anomaly.

00:23:54.310 --> 00:23:59.020
You don't see that with
traditionally white content,

00:23:59.020 --> 00:24:00.750
because it's so overwhelming.

00:24:00.750 --> 00:24:02.890
It's normal.

00:24:02.890 --> 00:24:07.540
If you normalize, then
maybe these arguments

00:24:07.540 --> 00:24:09.850
that are foolish stop.

00:24:09.850 --> 00:24:14.620
But it all goes to the top.

00:24:14.620 --> 00:24:19.240
When there are people of
color or allies or women

00:24:19.240 --> 00:24:23.670
up top making decisions, there
will be different results.

00:24:23.670 --> 00:24:24.790
DEVON FRANKLIN: Good.

00:24:24.790 --> 00:24:25.540
Excellent points.

00:24:28.260 --> 00:24:30.230
Debra what you were
just saying, it

00:24:30.230 --> 00:24:34.100
reminded me of that iconic
scene in Do The Right Thing

00:24:34.100 --> 00:24:38.600
when Mookie's having the
conversation at the pizza shop.

00:24:38.600 --> 00:24:41.660
And there's some black
faces, and they're like,

00:24:41.660 --> 00:24:42.855
oh no, those aren't niggas.

00:24:42.855 --> 00:24:43.730
You know what I mean?

00:24:43.730 --> 00:24:44.850
Like no.

00:24:44.850 --> 00:24:49.590
And it's this idea that just
because a person of color,

00:24:49.590 --> 00:24:53.360
a black man, black woman,
so on and so forth,

00:24:53.360 --> 00:24:55.340
becomes successful
at that level,

00:24:55.340 --> 00:24:57.110
oh they've transcended race.

00:24:57.110 --> 00:25:00.620
It is the most
fundamental lie that

00:25:00.620 --> 00:25:02.310
is perpetuated in our business.

00:25:02.310 --> 00:25:04.310
And then, Franklin, I
want to touch on something

00:25:04.310 --> 00:25:07.228
you said, which
was very profound.

00:25:07.228 --> 00:25:09.020
Listen, I know y'all
know me as a preacher.

00:25:09.020 --> 00:25:09.562
I don't know.

00:25:09.562 --> 00:25:12.117
Franklin, man, you got
a preacher in you man.

00:25:12.117 --> 00:25:13.700
You're quite an
evangelist my brother.

00:25:13.700 --> 00:25:15.620
FRANKLIN LEONARD:
It's the name, it

00:25:15.620 --> 00:25:16.950
comes from the Franklin thing.

00:25:16.950 --> 00:25:17.210
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:25:17.210 --> 00:25:19.220
DEVON FRANKLIN: That's
right, that's it.

00:25:19.220 --> 00:25:25.160
Because what you said, which is
so true, is this idea of value.

00:25:25.160 --> 00:25:30.480
Just because the color
of our skin is different,

00:25:30.480 --> 00:25:33.177
does that mean we
are less valuable?

00:25:33.177 --> 00:25:34.760
And when you think
about how Hollywood

00:25:34.760 --> 00:25:37.370
has perpetuated that
lie, that goes back

00:25:37.370 --> 00:25:39.730
to the days of slavery.

00:25:39.730 --> 00:25:45.270
When we as black people weren't
even considered a full person.

00:25:45.270 --> 00:25:49.970
So this is why we have to
continue to literally have

00:25:49.970 --> 00:25:54.710
this conversation, so that the
power structure and those that

00:25:54.710 --> 00:25:57.080
are in position can hear this.

00:25:57.080 --> 00:25:58.940
And when you really
hear it played back,

00:25:58.940 --> 00:26:00.290
it sounds ludicrous.

00:26:00.290 --> 00:26:02.870
Well no, of course, just because
you have a different color,

00:26:02.870 --> 00:26:06.160
or you're a different gender,
doesn't make you less valuable.

00:26:06.160 --> 00:26:08.690
But when you run
the P&amp;L, when you

00:26:08.690 --> 00:26:10.790
talk about who you can
put movies together with,

00:26:10.790 --> 00:26:12.582
their answer is, oh
well they don't travel.

00:26:12.582 --> 00:26:13.880
Well, why don't they travel?

00:26:13.880 --> 00:26:14.870
Because they aren't
supported, they

00:26:14.870 --> 00:26:15.230
aren't given the opportunity.

00:26:15.230 --> 00:26:17.313
FRANKLIN LEONARD: Because
you never marketed them.

00:26:17.313 --> 00:26:19.410
DEBRA MARTIN CHASE: Exactly.

00:26:19.410 --> 00:26:24.635
Beyonce is the biggest
music star in the world.

00:26:24.635 --> 00:26:25.760
FRANKLIN LEONARD: On earth.

00:26:25.760 --> 00:26:28.190
DEBRA MARTIN CHASE: On earth.

00:26:28.190 --> 00:26:32.360
And I remember being in
Kathmandu, Nepal, years ago

00:26:32.360 --> 00:26:34.370
and watching this kid
walk down the street

00:26:34.370 --> 00:26:35.990
with his pants hanging down.

00:26:35.990 --> 00:26:39.380
He had an old boom
box playing hip hop.

00:26:39.380 --> 00:26:46.500
And I'm like, we drive
culture around the world.

00:26:46.500 --> 00:26:49.900
How are movies not
being received?

00:26:49.900 --> 00:26:51.180
PHILLIP SUN: And also--

00:26:51.180 --> 00:26:51.680
go ahead.

00:26:51.680 --> 00:26:53.888
FRANKLIN LEONARD: I was just
going to say two things.

00:26:53.888 --> 00:26:56.180
There two moments for me
that really crystallized

00:26:56.180 --> 00:26:58.370
where Hollywood
misses it on race,

00:26:58.370 --> 00:27:00.630
and particularly as it
applies to black folks.

00:27:00.630 --> 00:27:03.560
One was actually relatively
early in my career.

00:27:03.560 --> 00:27:06.350
I remember, I think it
might have been Ride Along,

00:27:06.350 --> 00:27:11.947
and every Friday night
before the movies come out.

00:27:11.947 --> 00:27:14.030
And there are all of these
organizations that will

00:27:14.030 --> 00:27:16.210
try to forecast the box office.

00:27:16.210 --> 00:27:18.470
And they forecasted the
box office for Ride Along.

00:27:18.470 --> 00:27:20.980
The predictions were somewhere
in the low 20s, I think,

00:27:20.980 --> 00:27:22.430
or high teens.

00:27:22.430 --> 00:27:25.370
I remember tweeting,
it's a black movie.

00:27:25.370 --> 00:27:26.348
Take the over.

00:27:26.348 --> 00:27:27.890
Always take the over
on black movies,

00:27:27.890 --> 00:27:29.640
because they will
always underestimate us.

00:27:29.640 --> 00:27:31.160
And sure enough,
the movie came out,

00:27:31.160 --> 00:27:33.950
I think Ride Along opened
like mid 30s or something.

00:27:33.950 --> 00:27:36.320
And one of those
organizations tweeted,

00:27:36.320 --> 00:27:38.450
who could have foreseen
that Ride Along

00:27:38.450 --> 00:27:39.930
was going to do so well?

00:27:39.930 --> 00:27:40.670
And I responded.

00:27:40.670 --> 00:27:42.860
I was like, well here's
my receipt that I did.

00:27:42.860 --> 00:27:44.432
I always said take the over.

00:27:44.432 --> 00:27:45.890
And I was like,
and by the way, you

00:27:45.890 --> 00:27:47.390
should know that
if you look back

00:27:47.390 --> 00:27:49.940
at your last 10
black movies, you've

00:27:49.940 --> 00:27:52.700
been under every single time.

00:27:52.700 --> 00:27:54.405
And that may say something.

00:27:54.405 --> 00:27:56.030
And their response
was, well why do you

00:27:56.030 --> 00:27:57.440
have to make it about race?

00:27:57.440 --> 00:28:00.307
And I was like, listen,
it is about race, one.

00:28:00.307 --> 00:28:01.640
That's why I made it about race.

00:28:01.640 --> 00:28:05.000
But two, I'm actually less
offended as a black man

00:28:05.000 --> 00:28:07.820
than I am as a math nerd,
because your model is

00:28:07.820 --> 00:28:08.730
clearly wrong.

00:28:08.730 --> 00:28:11.570
And if you underestimate
something consistently,

00:28:11.570 --> 00:28:13.650
you should probably
fix your model.

00:28:13.650 --> 00:28:17.420
Now that's sort of fun from
a math nerd gotcha thing.

00:28:17.420 --> 00:28:19.550
But it goes to the core
of another issue, which

00:28:19.550 --> 00:28:23.210
is the studios themselves
are using the same formulas

00:28:23.210 --> 00:28:26.210
and algorithms to predict the
box office when they greenlight

00:28:26.210 --> 00:28:26.870
those movies.

00:28:26.870 --> 00:28:28.412
And if they assume
that they're going

00:28:28.412 --> 00:28:29.930
to make less money
than they are,

00:28:29.930 --> 00:28:31.670
they're less likely
to make those movies.

00:28:31.670 --> 00:28:33.920
They're less likely to give
them the financial support

00:28:33.920 --> 00:28:36.800
from a budget perspective and
from a marketing perspective.

00:28:36.800 --> 00:28:39.290
And you end up with
a circular process

00:28:39.290 --> 00:28:41.060
that results in less success.

00:28:41.060 --> 00:28:44.050
And it is remarkable and
a testament to the work

00:28:44.050 --> 00:28:47.313
that black folks and frankly
all kinds of diverse folks

00:28:47.313 --> 00:28:48.980
in this business are
doing, whether they

00:28:48.980 --> 00:28:51.750
be gay, whether they be
Asian, whether they be Latinx.

00:28:51.750 --> 00:28:53.330
That they continue
to over-perform,

00:28:53.330 --> 00:28:55.400
despite the lack of resources.

00:28:55.400 --> 00:28:57.680
It goes to show just
how much talent is there

00:28:57.680 --> 00:29:00.200
and just how much money there
is being left on the table

00:29:00.200 --> 00:29:02.420
by not giving them the
budgets they deserve

00:29:02.420 --> 00:29:04.760
and not giving them the
marketing support they deserve.

00:29:04.760 --> 00:29:06.218
Very quickly, the
second thing was,

00:29:06.218 --> 00:29:08.900
I was in Shanghai two years ago.

00:29:08.900 --> 00:29:12.230
And I think we all know that one
of the reasons that Hollywood

00:29:12.230 --> 00:29:16.040
specifically gives about racism
and black folks on screen

00:29:16.040 --> 00:29:18.680
is, well, we'd love to
make more black movies,

00:29:18.680 --> 00:29:21.680
but it's really hard to
sell those movies in China

00:29:21.680 --> 00:29:22.980
specifically.

00:29:22.980 --> 00:29:24.980
And it's a huge market
and it's really important

00:29:24.980 --> 00:29:27.872
that we are successful there.

00:29:27.872 --> 00:29:30.080
I was walking back to my
hotel one night in Shanghai,

00:29:30.080 --> 00:29:35.030
and a giant billboard above
the Tomorrow Square Marriott

00:29:35.030 --> 00:29:37.490
was a billboard for
the new Apple iPhone

00:29:37.490 --> 00:29:41.260
with a dark-skinned black
woman on the Billboard.

00:29:41.260 --> 00:29:46.080
Now Apple clearly thinks they
can sell iPhones in China

00:29:46.080 --> 00:29:48.440
with black faces.

00:29:48.440 --> 00:29:50.420
And somehow Hollywood
doesn't think they

00:29:50.420 --> 00:29:53.010
can sell black faces in movies.

00:29:53.010 --> 00:29:54.830
If I had to choose
between who's done

00:29:54.830 --> 00:29:57.950
a more consistent job of selling
their products effectively

00:29:57.950 --> 00:30:01.730
around the world, I'm
picking Apple every time.

00:30:01.730 --> 00:30:05.390
And the core of that is,
it's not just racist to say,

00:30:05.390 --> 00:30:07.850
oh we can't sell
black people abroad.

00:30:07.850 --> 00:30:11.330
It's specifically saying, no we
can't sell black people abroad,

00:30:11.330 --> 00:30:13.910
because the Chinese are
more racist than we are.

00:30:13.910 --> 00:30:17.390
It's actually a double racism,
because you're characterizing

00:30:17.390 --> 00:30:20.280
not only black folks and
their value around the world,

00:30:20.280 --> 00:30:22.940
but also Chinese people
and the Chinese community,

00:30:22.940 --> 00:30:25.670
as somehow worse
than the Westerners

00:30:25.670 --> 00:30:28.743
that created anti-blackness
in the first place.

00:30:28.743 --> 00:30:30.160
PHILLIP SUN: Yeah
it's deflecting.

00:30:30.160 --> 00:30:31.285
It's absolutely deflecting.

00:30:31.285 --> 00:30:32.620
FRANKLIN LEONARD: Yeah.

00:30:32.620 --> 00:30:36.790
Which is why, for me, all of
us have got to get together,

00:30:36.790 --> 00:30:39.770
because if all of us
aren't at the party,

00:30:39.770 --> 00:30:41.260
it's not a good party.

00:30:41.260 --> 00:30:44.980
I'm interested in a culture
that has everybody represented.

00:30:44.980 --> 00:30:46.635
I want the Asian
community there.

00:30:46.635 --> 00:30:48.010
I want the black
community there.

00:30:48.010 --> 00:30:49.110
I want the Latinx
community there.

00:30:49.110 --> 00:30:50.770
I want the LGBTQ
community there.

00:30:50.770 --> 00:30:52.520
I want the Middle
Eastern community there.

00:30:52.520 --> 00:30:53.410
I want women there.

00:30:53.410 --> 00:30:54.740
The food's going to be better.

00:30:54.740 --> 00:30:55.930
Music's going to be better.

00:30:55.930 --> 00:30:57.190
The dance floor is
going to be lit,

00:30:57.190 --> 00:30:57.890
and we're all going
to have a great time.

00:30:57.890 --> 00:30:59.510
DEBRA MARTIN CHASE: It's
the world we live in.

00:30:59.510 --> 00:31:00.510
PHILLIP SUN: Absolutely.

00:31:00.510 --> 00:31:03.100
DEBRA MARTIN CHASE:
Listen, we love

00:31:03.100 --> 00:31:06.610
movies, that's why we're
here and we work so hard.

00:31:06.610 --> 00:31:09.340
But the movies are
to represent what's

00:31:09.340 --> 00:31:13.990
going on in society, in
the world at a given time.

00:31:13.990 --> 00:31:18.280
And if we're not capturing
the globalization,

00:31:18.280 --> 00:31:22.040
the multicultural thing
that's going on right now,

00:31:22.040 --> 00:31:26.905
then we're dis-serving
the art of cinema.

00:31:29.600 --> 00:31:31.150
It's interesting.

00:31:31.150 --> 00:31:34.220
TV has had a
different trajectory.

00:31:34.220 --> 00:31:40.850
And in some ways, TV has kind
of led this current phase--

00:31:40.850 --> 00:31:44.350
and I don't think it's a
phase, I think it's a turn--

00:31:44.350 --> 00:31:46.240
to embrace diversity.

00:31:46.240 --> 00:31:50.350
And Phil you made
the point of it

00:31:50.350 --> 00:31:51.670
goes with the decision-makers.

00:31:51.670 --> 00:31:53.440
Well, there was a
moment in time that

00:31:53.440 --> 00:31:56.920
really didn't get
publicized much,

00:31:56.920 --> 00:32:02.980
where the head of drama at
every major network was black.

00:32:02.980 --> 00:32:04.880
You know it was Channing.

00:32:04.880 --> 00:32:07.700
It was Terence Carter.

00:32:07.700 --> 00:32:10.670
It was my girl Pearlena.

00:32:10.670 --> 00:32:15.530
And that's when Scandal--

00:32:15.530 --> 00:32:17.990
they didn't want to cast black.

00:32:17.990 --> 00:32:22.190
But you had Shonda coming off of
Grey's Anatomy with some juice.

00:32:22.190 --> 00:32:24.290
You had Channing
as head of drama.

00:32:24.290 --> 00:32:26.690
And you had the fact that
the character was based

00:32:26.690 --> 00:32:28.810
on Judy Smith, a black woman.

00:32:28.810 --> 00:32:30.560
So people knew
that they would get

00:32:30.560 --> 00:32:32.990
killed if they cast her white.

00:32:32.990 --> 00:32:36.440
So it was with trepidation
that the decision was

00:32:36.440 --> 00:32:38.220
made to cast Kerry Washington.

00:32:38.220 --> 00:32:40.083
And you know, boom.

00:32:40.083 --> 00:32:41.900
Then Viola, boom.

00:32:41.900 --> 00:32:45.770
And then Lee Daniels with
Empire took it over the top.

00:32:45.770 --> 00:32:50.490
The highest-rated debut in
a decade, blah, blah, blah.

00:32:50.490 --> 00:32:53.923
And that helped the rest of the
industry say, oh wait a minute.

00:32:53.923 --> 00:32:55.340
PHILLIP SUN: I
think that's right.

00:32:55.340 --> 00:32:57.560
I think also on top
of all of that right,

00:32:57.560 --> 00:33:03.570
going back to what Franklin said
before about traveling abroad,

00:33:03.570 --> 00:33:06.770
and it works if you
market it correctly.

00:33:06.770 --> 00:33:09.560
I think that not
only decision-making

00:33:09.560 --> 00:33:12.200
processes of content,
but marketing dollars,

00:33:12.200 --> 00:33:14.120
and who's marketing.

00:33:14.120 --> 00:33:16.190
Who's making decisions
on marketing?

00:33:16.190 --> 00:33:21.470
We're seeing such a trend now
of black artists, Asian artists,

00:33:21.470 --> 00:33:24.200
whomever, whatever talent,
multicultural talent,

00:33:24.200 --> 00:33:26.770
having their own
marketing firms.

00:33:26.770 --> 00:33:29.790
It's because they're not being
listened to at the studio

00:33:29.790 --> 00:33:30.290
level.

00:33:30.290 --> 00:33:34.340
They only know how to market
the way that they traditionally

00:33:34.340 --> 00:33:36.770
have marketed, and that's
not speaking to the audience

00:33:36.770 --> 00:33:38.510
that the artist is actually
trying to speak to,

00:33:38.510 --> 00:33:40.052
and it's not speaking
to the audience

00:33:40.052 --> 00:33:43.130
in the way the content is
trying to speak to them.

00:33:43.130 --> 00:33:48.620
And so you can't market
Ride Along necessarily

00:33:48.620 --> 00:33:52.960
with a white lens, and expect
it to land in the community.

00:33:52.960 --> 00:33:54.080
That doesn't work.

00:33:54.080 --> 00:33:56.180
It sets it up for failure.

00:33:56.180 --> 00:34:02.750
All these pieces that are again
in a perfectly formed machine,

00:34:02.750 --> 00:34:06.650
just need to evolve to
be used in the way that

00:34:06.650 --> 00:34:08.690
reflects the world today.

00:34:08.690 --> 00:34:09.949
And it will work.

00:34:09.949 --> 00:34:13.659
But until we make those
decision-makers reflect

00:34:13.659 --> 00:34:15.230
more of what the
world looks like,

00:34:15.230 --> 00:34:17.370
we're going to be at a deficit.

00:34:17.370 --> 00:34:18.909
DEVON FRANKLIN: So speaking of--

00:34:18.909 --> 00:34:19.659
oh go ahead Frank.

00:34:19.659 --> 00:34:19.889
FRANKLIN LEONARD: I
was just going to say,

00:34:19.889 --> 00:34:22.239
one thing that does
excite me about that film,

00:34:22.239 --> 00:34:24.290
is that at the end
of the day, given

00:34:24.290 --> 00:34:27.350
the way the world is working
and given technology,

00:34:27.350 --> 00:34:29.690
and one upside of the
pandemic, to the extent

00:34:29.690 --> 00:34:31.909
that we can talk about
that, is that because

00:34:31.909 --> 00:34:35.929
of the rapid change, those folks
who do lead those companies who

00:34:35.929 --> 00:34:39.350
fail to understand
where things are going,

00:34:39.350 --> 00:34:41.670
they have really two options.

00:34:41.670 --> 00:34:43.150
One is to do nothing.

00:34:43.150 --> 00:34:46.130
Continue to do what they
do and run their companies

00:34:46.130 --> 00:34:47.449
into the ground.

00:34:47.449 --> 00:34:50.389
Or find somebody
who does understand

00:34:50.389 --> 00:34:52.429
the culture as it
exists now, and then

00:34:52.429 --> 00:34:53.760
have a chance of success.

00:34:53.760 --> 00:34:57.080
And so in many ways,
it is the shareholders.

00:34:57.080 --> 00:34:59.598
It is the boards of
these companies who

00:34:59.598 --> 00:35:01.640
need to look around at
the people who are running

00:35:01.640 --> 00:35:04.880
these things and say,
is this the best person

00:35:04.880 --> 00:35:06.960
to take us through this moment?

00:35:06.960 --> 00:35:10.610
And if they're not, you
have a fiduciary obligation

00:35:10.610 --> 00:35:13.310
to your company and
to your shareholders

00:35:13.310 --> 00:35:17.360
to remove them and replace
them with someone who does.

00:35:17.360 --> 00:35:22.103
And that's just the way it is.

00:35:22.103 --> 00:35:24.270
PHILLIP SUN: Could I piggyback
on that for a second?

00:35:24.270 --> 00:35:24.770
FRANKLIN LEONARD: Of course.

00:35:24.770 --> 00:35:26.312
PHILLIP SUN: That's
absolutely right,

00:35:26.312 --> 00:35:29.390
and I also want to turn it on
us a little bit as a community.

00:35:29.390 --> 00:35:34.490
When those executives or our
friends get those positions,

00:35:34.490 --> 00:35:37.700
we have to back them
as a unified group.

00:35:37.700 --> 00:35:40.220
There is not one seat
at the table anymore.

00:35:40.220 --> 00:35:41.990
Build your own table.

00:35:41.990 --> 00:35:42.770
They will come.

00:35:42.770 --> 00:35:44.660
Trust me, it's cooler there.

00:35:44.660 --> 00:35:48.080
It's just the culture is there.

00:35:48.080 --> 00:35:51.440
We have to have that
together mentality,

00:35:51.440 --> 00:35:56.240
because the truth is,
any cultural group is not

00:35:56.240 --> 00:35:58.400
strong enough to stand
up to the majority.

00:35:58.400 --> 00:35:59.110
Singular group.

00:35:59.110 --> 00:36:00.760
They're just not.

00:36:00.760 --> 00:36:02.870
The whole reason I
paired up with Charles,

00:36:02.870 --> 00:36:06.415
other than he's my mentor, is
time to send a message, too.

00:36:06.415 --> 00:36:08.630
It's like, yes.

00:36:08.630 --> 00:36:12.080
I had opportunities elsewhere
to be probably, ultimately

00:36:12.080 --> 00:36:13.610
the token.

00:36:13.610 --> 00:36:17.120
Or I can link arms
with my mentor

00:36:17.120 --> 00:36:20.150
and be stronger together.

00:36:20.150 --> 00:36:22.820
To stand on the shoulders
of what he's already built,

00:36:22.820 --> 00:36:26.240
and to continue to rise
and pull everyone with us.

00:36:26.240 --> 00:36:29.050
I think one of the coolest
things, Theresa Kang

00:36:29.050 --> 00:36:32.540
and myself, we represented
Lena Waithe together

00:36:32.540 --> 00:36:34.450
when we were at WME.

00:36:34.450 --> 00:36:38.060
And we brought her to Crazy
Rich Asians, and she she

00:36:38.060 --> 00:36:40.870
posted about Crazy Rich Asians.

00:36:40.870 --> 00:36:43.000
And at the time,
the Asian community

00:36:43.000 --> 00:36:45.900
was like, wow, thanks.

00:36:45.900 --> 00:36:48.150
Oh, that's incredible.

00:36:48.150 --> 00:36:50.160
But on the flip
side, there's also

00:36:50.160 --> 00:36:52.290
some community
members who were like,

00:36:52.290 --> 00:36:54.060
why are you supporting them?

00:36:54.060 --> 00:36:55.180
They don't support us.

00:36:55.180 --> 00:36:56.690
We have to build that bridge.

00:36:56.690 --> 00:36:58.500
DEBRA MARTIN CHASE: Absolutely.

00:36:58.500 --> 00:37:00.090
PHILLIP SUN: Because
if I'm standing

00:37:00.090 --> 00:37:02.850
by Deb, Devon,
and Franklin, they

00:37:02.850 --> 00:37:06.780
can't say it's a Chinese thing.

00:37:06.780 --> 00:37:09.180
They can't pigeonhole me.

00:37:09.180 --> 00:37:12.030
And if anyone's standing
by Deb, of us three,

00:37:12.030 --> 00:37:15.090
they can't just say it's
a powerful woman thing.

00:37:15.090 --> 00:37:15.840
Because I'm there.

00:37:15.840 --> 00:37:17.132
FRANKLIN LEONARD: That's right.

00:37:17.132 --> 00:37:18.360
PHILLIP SUN: You're there.

00:37:18.360 --> 00:37:21.090
You've got to play a little
bit of offense through defense.

00:37:21.090 --> 00:37:23.100
Where are they going
to poke the holes?

00:37:23.100 --> 00:37:23.600
Cover up.

00:37:23.600 --> 00:37:25.350
DEBRA MARTIN CHASE:
Well it's the oldest--

00:37:25.350 --> 00:37:27.140
look, I don't want
to get into politics,

00:37:27.140 --> 00:37:32.110
but divide and conquer
is one of the oldest

00:37:32.110 --> 00:37:33.950
strategies in the book.

00:37:33.950 --> 00:37:35.790
And we've got to take control.

00:37:35.790 --> 00:37:37.290
You're absolutely right Phil.

00:37:37.290 --> 00:37:39.450
DEVON FRANKLIN: 1,000%.

00:37:39.450 --> 00:37:42.950
On that part,
we're acknowledging

00:37:42.950 --> 00:37:45.480
the challenges in the
industry and the truth

00:37:45.480 --> 00:37:48.360
of the industry in
a way that I believe

00:37:48.360 --> 00:37:50.330
anyone watching
this conversation

00:37:50.330 --> 00:37:53.790
will be riveted by it, and
their mind will be expanded.

00:37:53.790 --> 00:37:56.790
And I think it will also
produce, I'm hoping,

00:37:56.790 --> 00:37:59.560
more of a desire
to create change.

00:37:59.560 --> 00:38:01.730
I want to talk about
something which

00:38:01.730 --> 00:38:03.030
we don't talk about a lot.

00:38:03.030 --> 00:38:06.840
But I know that each one of
us has had to deal with it.

00:38:06.840 --> 00:38:08.820
Frustration.

00:38:08.820 --> 00:38:11.670
When we acknowledge the
reality of the systems

00:38:11.670 --> 00:38:16.150
that we all have to deal with in
order to further our business,

00:38:16.150 --> 00:38:17.237
live our dreams.

00:38:17.237 --> 00:38:19.320
I just want each one of
you just to take a moment.

00:38:19.320 --> 00:38:21.750
How do you deal
with frustration?

00:38:21.750 --> 00:38:25.390
How do you not just say,
forget it, I'm done?

00:38:25.390 --> 00:38:26.410
I can't do it anymore.

00:38:26.410 --> 00:38:27.570
I'm tired.

00:38:27.570 --> 00:38:29.538
How do you handle frustration?

00:38:29.538 --> 00:38:32.080
Franklin, I want to start with
you, and then we'll go to Deb,

00:38:32.080 --> 00:38:34.090
and then we'll go to Phil.

00:38:34.090 --> 00:38:37.190
FRANKLIN LEONARD: Most recently
I've been running a lot.

00:38:37.190 --> 00:38:39.730
I find it very difficult
to focus on my frustrations

00:38:39.730 --> 00:38:42.352
when I'm struggling to breathe,
so that's very helpful.

00:38:42.352 --> 00:38:43.810
PHILLIP SUN: You
look very slender.

00:38:43.810 --> 00:38:46.730
FRANKLIN LEONARD: The pandemic's
been good for me in that regard

00:38:46.730 --> 00:38:48.580
too.

00:38:48.580 --> 00:38:51.340
Look, I think for
me, it comes down

00:38:51.340 --> 00:38:55.510
to remembering how lucky I am
that these are my frustrations.

00:38:55.510 --> 00:38:57.840
And that there are folks who--

00:38:57.840 --> 00:39:02.170
my grandfather never
finished the fifth grade

00:39:02.170 --> 00:39:03.490
in west central Georgia.

00:39:03.490 --> 00:39:06.580
His father was born a slave
in west central Georgia.

00:39:06.580 --> 00:39:08.620
And so when I think
about the frustrations

00:39:08.620 --> 00:39:11.800
that I have trying to
navigate the film business,

00:39:11.800 --> 00:39:13.780
knowing that I have
a roof over my head

00:39:13.780 --> 00:39:18.370
and I can afford a nice meal,
it becomes a lot more difficult

00:39:18.370 --> 00:39:22.872
for me to be overcome by the
frustrations that I have.

00:39:22.872 --> 00:39:24.580
Because I can look in
the mirror and say,

00:39:24.580 --> 00:39:27.940
yeah this is really frustrating,
and it is really difficult.

00:39:27.940 --> 00:39:31.840
But people that
have come before me

00:39:31.840 --> 00:39:36.300
have dealt with worse, under
more adverse circumstances.

00:39:36.300 --> 00:39:38.750
And I will be
damned if I let less

00:39:38.750 --> 00:39:41.270
than they had to endure
prevent me from trying

00:39:41.270 --> 00:39:42.960
to accomplish what they did.

00:39:42.960 --> 00:39:44.960
Because I'm already
standing on their shoulders,

00:39:44.960 --> 00:39:46.940
I can at least match their work.

00:39:46.940 --> 00:39:47.893
And it helps.

00:39:47.893 --> 00:39:49.310
It doesn't remove
the frustration,

00:39:49.310 --> 00:39:52.650
but it definitely increases
my capacity to deal with it.

00:39:52.650 --> 00:39:54.800
And I think it's
a debt that I owe

00:39:54.800 --> 00:39:58.400
everyone that came before me
to try to endure and find ways

00:39:58.400 --> 00:39:58.940
to cope.

00:39:58.940 --> 00:40:00.565
But I also think it's
really important,

00:40:00.565 --> 00:40:02.390
and this is a self care thing.

00:40:02.390 --> 00:40:04.595
I made a joke about the
running, but it's true.

00:40:04.595 --> 00:40:06.470
Sometimes you just have
to pull the thing out

00:40:06.470 --> 00:40:08.690
of the socket for a minute.

00:40:08.690 --> 00:40:10.460
Go for a run.

00:40:10.460 --> 00:40:12.230
Eat some unhealthy food.

00:40:12.230 --> 00:40:15.440
Watch an old Richard
Pryor special.

00:40:15.440 --> 00:40:19.760
And just allow your brain to not
feel the anxiety and the stress

00:40:19.760 --> 00:40:21.800
and the frustration.

00:40:21.800 --> 00:40:24.140
Get a good night's sleep,
and wake up the next morning

00:40:24.140 --> 00:40:25.040
ready to fight again.

00:40:25.040 --> 00:40:27.482
Because you can't win every
day, you don't win every game.

00:40:27.482 --> 00:40:28.940
Like if I played
any sport, they're

00:40:28.940 --> 00:40:31.790
are going to be days when
you get taken to town,

00:40:31.790 --> 00:40:33.830
and you go back to
the locker room.

00:40:33.830 --> 00:40:35.840
And you're like, man forget it.

00:40:35.840 --> 00:40:38.060
I shouldn't even be
playing this game.

00:40:38.060 --> 00:40:40.813
But there's another game on
the schedule, and you wake up

00:40:40.813 --> 00:40:42.230
and you keep playing
the schedule.

00:40:42.230 --> 00:40:44.490
And the hope is that you
win more than you lose.

00:40:44.490 --> 00:40:46.490
And you make the playoffs,
and you have a chance

00:40:46.490 --> 00:40:47.900
to take it to game seven.

00:40:47.900 --> 00:40:52.200
And it's a cliche metaphor,
but I think it's really true.

00:40:52.200 --> 00:40:54.108
You can't take
yourself too seriously,

00:40:54.108 --> 00:40:56.150
and you can't think that
anything is apocalyptic.

00:40:56.150 --> 00:40:59.060
Because for all the
seriousness of the work

00:40:59.060 --> 00:41:01.670
that we do, and it
is serious, it's

00:41:01.670 --> 00:41:04.590
not life or death
in the short term.

00:41:04.590 --> 00:41:08.930
And so if you can take a look
at a long enough timeline,

00:41:08.930 --> 00:41:10.940
we all should be able to win.

00:41:10.940 --> 00:41:13.070
And I call Phil.

00:41:13.070 --> 00:41:14.570
I email you Devon.

00:41:14.570 --> 00:41:19.740
I run into Deb, and you
tell me it's going to be OK.

00:41:19.740 --> 00:41:22.000
And that makes it easier.

00:41:22.000 --> 00:41:23.280
DEVON FRANKLIN: Love that.

00:41:23.280 --> 00:41:24.740
Debra?

00:41:24.740 --> 00:41:28.730
DEBRA MARTIN CHASE: You
know, when I was growing up,

00:41:28.730 --> 00:41:31.820
I never saw people on
screen that looked like me.

00:41:31.820 --> 00:41:35.450
And I was a kid that grew
up with film and television.

00:41:35.450 --> 00:41:37.070
My dad was a huge movie buff.

00:41:37.070 --> 00:41:40.790
And so I got into this
business to change that.

00:41:40.790 --> 00:41:43.040
To change the images on screen.

00:41:43.040 --> 00:41:47.010
To change the
narrative, if you will,

00:41:47.010 --> 00:41:51.240
about women and women of
color and people of color.

00:41:51.240 --> 00:41:53.810
So I had a higher mission.

00:41:53.810 --> 00:41:55.190
I also was a lawyer.

00:41:55.190 --> 00:41:56.540
I practiced law.

00:41:56.540 --> 00:42:02.860
So I wasn't defined
by the business.

00:42:02.860 --> 00:42:07.220
And so, I realized that I
just kept moving forward.

00:42:07.220 --> 00:42:10.510
I would deflect
things that happened.

00:42:13.370 --> 00:42:14.480
I'm on a mission.

00:42:14.480 --> 00:42:16.640
That's your problem.

00:42:16.640 --> 00:42:19.130
I was back in the
days when literally, I

00:42:19.130 --> 00:42:21.800
would walk in to a
meeting, and the guy

00:42:21.800 --> 00:42:23.690
would ask me to go get coffee.

00:42:23.690 --> 00:42:26.230
Those days, because
he just said,

00:42:26.230 --> 00:42:29.810
black woman, she'd
be lucky to be

00:42:29.810 --> 00:42:34.130
an assistant at a studio,
much less an executive.

00:42:34.130 --> 00:42:39.770
And what I realized is that
I internalized a lot of that.

00:42:39.770 --> 00:42:43.180
Because it doesn't just go away.

00:42:43.180 --> 00:42:46.930
You just suck it up, you just
put it down and say, look I

00:42:46.930 --> 00:42:48.160
have a higher purpose here.

00:42:51.520 --> 00:42:53.200
When I became the
first black woman

00:42:53.200 --> 00:42:56.560
to have a producing
deal at a studio,

00:42:56.560 --> 00:42:59.770
that was really important to me.

00:42:59.770 --> 00:43:04.330
Not in and of itself, but just
because it was a sign for me

00:43:04.330 --> 00:43:07.650
that the mission was working.

00:43:07.650 --> 00:43:12.850
But I have to tell you, a few
years ago I was ready to quit.

00:43:12.850 --> 00:43:18.460
It was that period of time where
Hollywood was not even thinking

00:43:18.460 --> 00:43:21.633
about making movies about
women or people of color.

00:43:21.633 --> 00:43:23.800
And obviously, I've done a
little bit of everything,

00:43:23.800 --> 00:43:25.270
but that's my sweet spot.

00:43:25.270 --> 00:43:28.000
That's the mission
that I started with,

00:43:28.000 --> 00:43:31.180
and that was a real
motivation for me.

00:43:31.180 --> 00:43:32.860
And I would go into
people's offices

00:43:32.860 --> 00:43:34.330
and their eyes would glaze over.

00:43:36.880 --> 00:43:39.270
They just weren't interested.

00:43:39.270 --> 00:43:41.980
And then you start
throwing stuff up

00:43:41.980 --> 00:43:44.350
against the wall
that doesn't really

00:43:44.350 --> 00:43:46.390
mean anything to
you in here, which

00:43:46.390 --> 00:43:49.210
is where I live as a filmmaker.

00:43:49.210 --> 00:43:54.500
And it wasn't sticking, because
it just wasn't, you know.

00:43:54.500 --> 00:43:58.750
And honestly, I said to
myself, well, maybe I'm done.

00:43:58.750 --> 00:44:02.240
Maybe I have served my purpose.

00:44:02.240 --> 00:44:07.510
And I started looking at
maybe I'd take this and go

00:44:07.510 --> 00:44:08.580
to Wall Street.

00:44:08.580 --> 00:44:10.660
So I started looking
around, because I

00:44:10.660 --> 00:44:15.940
was like, I don't want
to live like this,

00:44:15.940 --> 00:44:18.160
not feeling fulfilled
and satisfied.

00:44:18.160 --> 00:44:24.910
And then honestly, Harriet,
which took me six years,

00:44:24.910 --> 00:44:28.060
but started gaining momentum.

00:44:28.060 --> 00:44:32.590
And in doing
Harriet, I remembered

00:44:32.590 --> 00:44:35.035
why I wanted to make movies.

00:44:35.035 --> 00:44:35.910
DEVON FRANKLIN: Nice.

00:44:35.910 --> 00:44:38.470
DEBRA MARTIN CHASE:
And thank God, I just

00:44:38.470 --> 00:44:43.650
lasted til the tide changed.

00:44:43.650 --> 00:44:47.065
And all of a sudden,
because we still

00:44:47.065 --> 00:44:48.790
have such a long
way to go, but this

00:44:48.790 --> 00:44:50.410
is the best that it's been.

00:44:50.410 --> 00:44:55.510
In terms of recognizing
the value of black product

00:44:55.510 --> 00:44:59.130
and talent, since I've
been in the business,

00:44:59.130 --> 00:45:00.210
and in a real way.

00:45:00.210 --> 00:45:03.700
And not black, just
multicultural talent.

00:45:03.700 --> 00:45:08.080
From Crazy Rich Asians to
Black Panther and whatever.

00:45:08.080 --> 00:45:10.840
So I'm happy I stayed.

00:45:10.840 --> 00:45:11.600
I'm--

00:45:11.600 --> 00:45:12.370
DEVON FRANKLIN: So are we.

00:45:12.370 --> 00:45:13.110
DEBRA MARTIN CHASE:
We're all benefiting.

00:45:13.110 --> 00:45:13.630
FRANKLIN LEONARD:
I was going to say,

00:45:13.630 --> 00:45:15.340
I'm happy you stayed too Debra.

00:45:15.340 --> 00:45:17.673
I don't know if I could have
handled you leaving either.

00:45:17.673 --> 00:45:19.360
DEVON FRANKLIN: Lord of mercy.

00:45:19.360 --> 00:45:19.902
I don't know.

00:45:19.902 --> 00:45:22.110
I don't know if any of us
could have dealt with that.

00:45:22.110 --> 00:45:23.620
DEBRA MARTIN CHASE:
Seriously, I had

00:45:23.620 --> 00:45:26.677
one foot way out of the door.

00:45:26.677 --> 00:45:27.510
DEVON FRANKLIN: Wow.

00:45:27.510 --> 00:45:28.570
PHILLIP SUN: [INAUDIBLE]

00:45:28.570 --> 00:45:30.028
DEVON FRANKLIN:
But it's a blessing

00:45:30.028 --> 00:45:33.770
that you saw that you had to
be here and you persevered.

00:45:33.770 --> 00:45:36.250
And obviously
Harriet was amazing,

00:45:36.250 --> 00:45:38.500
and all the things you're
doing in film and television

00:45:38.500 --> 00:45:39.555
are incredible.

00:45:39.555 --> 00:45:40.930
I know our time
is getting short,

00:45:40.930 --> 00:45:42.305
but Phil I want
you just to touch

00:45:42.305 --> 00:45:44.650
on this quickly if you could.

00:45:44.650 --> 00:45:48.048
How do you handle frustration?

00:45:48.048 --> 00:45:49.840
PHILLIP SUN: I piggyback
a bit on Franklin.

00:45:49.840 --> 00:45:52.660
Definitely exercise.

00:45:52.660 --> 00:45:56.530
But it comes down to, my
life changed nine months ago

00:45:56.530 --> 00:45:59.730
when I had my son.

00:45:59.730 --> 00:46:03.137
As you become a
parent, you realize

00:46:03.137 --> 00:46:05.095
that it's not about you,
and it's about legacy.

00:46:08.320 --> 00:46:12.940
He's half Chinese, half Jewish.

00:46:12.940 --> 00:46:15.690
If he grows up anything
like me, in the environments

00:46:15.690 --> 00:46:19.240
that I grew up in, he'll
be seen as different.

00:46:19.240 --> 00:46:22.040
He'll be treated as such.

00:46:22.040 --> 00:46:24.070
And that doesn't
sit well with me.

00:46:24.070 --> 00:46:28.770
So it's more of
a, how am I going

00:46:28.770 --> 00:46:31.950
to do my part to
change this world,

00:46:31.950 --> 00:46:34.260
this industry this world?

00:46:34.260 --> 00:46:39.030
So that he feels like he
belongs, because he does.

00:46:39.030 --> 00:46:45.440
And more alongside of that is
the future of the industry.

00:46:45.440 --> 00:46:48.620
If I get frustrated
and I quit, then it's

00:46:48.620 --> 00:46:51.940
not going to get any better
for the Asian kid, black kid,

00:46:51.940 --> 00:46:55.010
woman who's coming up the ranks.

00:46:55.010 --> 00:46:57.500
If they can't look up
and see someone did it.

00:46:57.500 --> 00:46:59.930
So Frank and I have had
this conversation before.

00:46:59.930 --> 00:47:03.710
Maybe it's our duty
in our lifetime

00:47:03.710 --> 00:47:07.160
to take this on, and
push the ball forward.

00:47:07.160 --> 00:47:11.870
And Deb certainly has
done it, for all of us.

00:47:11.870 --> 00:47:14.660
And we will continue to push it.

00:47:14.660 --> 00:47:18.200
But the alternative is
not acceptable to me,

00:47:18.200 --> 00:47:20.210
which is quitting.

00:47:20.210 --> 00:47:23.390
The alternative is
to allow the system

00:47:23.390 --> 00:47:27.290
to beat me, which is
not going to happen.

00:47:27.290 --> 00:47:31.070
And then I look at my
son, my family, and then

00:47:31.070 --> 00:47:34.380
the future generation
of executives,

00:47:34.380 --> 00:47:38.630
specifically in representation
for me, and there is no quit.

00:47:38.630 --> 00:47:40.490
Because you've got to
make sure that they

00:47:40.490 --> 00:47:43.040
can stand on our shoulders.

00:47:43.040 --> 00:47:44.990
And keep going.

00:47:44.990 --> 00:47:47.890
DEVON FRANKLIN: Devon, you
should answer that question.

00:47:47.890 --> 00:47:49.440
PHILLIP SUN: Yeah dude.

00:47:49.440 --> 00:47:51.690
DEBRA MARTIN CHASE: With all
that you've accomplished.

00:47:51.690 --> 00:47:54.840
You're a governor of the
Motion Picture Academy.

00:47:54.840 --> 00:47:57.010
We weren't even getting in
the door a few years ago

00:47:57.010 --> 00:47:58.477
to be a member.

00:47:58.477 --> 00:48:00.060
DEVON FRANKLIN: Well
thank you, Debra.

00:48:00.060 --> 00:48:02.230
This is why you are who you are.

00:48:02.230 --> 00:48:08.225
Well I'll answer briefly,
and tie it into our wrap up.

00:48:08.225 --> 00:48:10.020
I deal with frustration.

00:48:10.020 --> 00:48:13.440
My antidote for
frustration is faith.

00:48:13.440 --> 00:48:17.000
There have been times, when
I was an executive, man

00:48:17.000 --> 00:48:18.750
I'd be in my office crying.

00:48:18.750 --> 00:48:21.060
Because I'm like,
there's got to be more.

00:48:21.060 --> 00:48:22.080
I don't understand.

00:48:22.080 --> 00:48:26.672
I'm playing by the rules,
but the score is different.

00:48:26.672 --> 00:48:27.630
And I don't understand.

00:48:27.630 --> 00:48:28.650
I don't know what's going on.

00:48:28.650 --> 00:48:29.520
I have these dreams.

00:48:29.520 --> 00:48:30.312
I have these hopes.

00:48:30.312 --> 00:48:32.700
I'm getting in early,
I'm staying late.

00:48:32.700 --> 00:48:34.570
But where's the progress?

00:48:34.570 --> 00:48:38.000
And so, in those
darkest moments,

00:48:38.000 --> 00:48:40.980
and because we care
so much, I know

00:48:40.980 --> 00:48:44.033
I'm not exclusive in that
regard, we internalize.

00:48:44.033 --> 00:48:46.200
So when things aren't
necessarily going right for us

00:48:46.200 --> 00:48:48.840
in this business, things aren't
initially going right for us

00:48:48.840 --> 00:48:49.960
personally.

00:48:49.960 --> 00:48:51.888
And so for me, I
would internalize.

00:48:51.888 --> 00:48:53.430
And I've learned to
separate the two,

00:48:53.430 --> 00:48:56.430
but at the time, especially,
when I was the most frustrated,

00:48:56.430 --> 00:48:57.900
I was tethering the two.

00:48:57.900 --> 00:49:00.780
My value with my
progress in the business.

00:49:00.780 --> 00:49:03.120
And I was looking in
the mirror and not

00:49:03.120 --> 00:49:04.860
liking who looked back at me.

00:49:04.860 --> 00:49:08.680
Questioning, wondering,
doubting, being insecure.

00:49:08.680 --> 00:49:11.370
And so I had to have faith.

00:49:11.370 --> 00:49:14.250
I had to say, OK, I know
God has given me a vision.

00:49:14.250 --> 00:49:15.630
I have to rely on that vision.

00:49:15.630 --> 00:49:17.340
The same way we go
to the movie theater

00:49:17.340 --> 00:49:20.640
and we see the trailer for
a movie that we want to see,

00:49:20.640 --> 00:49:22.410
I had to keep closing
my eyes and seeing

00:49:22.410 --> 00:49:24.570
the trailer of the
movie of my life play

00:49:24.570 --> 00:49:25.860
on the screen in my mind.

00:49:25.860 --> 00:49:29.340
And that honestly is what has
helped me navigate frustration,

00:49:29.340 --> 00:49:30.960
because I go on the inside.

00:49:30.960 --> 00:49:32.010
I listen, and I see.

00:49:32.010 --> 00:49:34.202
And I say, oh OK,
I can see that.

00:49:34.202 --> 00:49:36.660
And that gives me enough energy
and strength to get back up

00:49:36.660 --> 00:49:38.610
and fight and get
back in the game

00:49:38.610 --> 00:49:41.610
and have conversations
and push and press.

00:49:41.610 --> 00:49:43.840
And to Phil's point, not quit.

00:49:43.840 --> 00:49:46.260
And so that that's how I've
dealt with frustration,

00:49:46.260 --> 00:49:49.380
and that's how I continue to
deal with it, through faith.

00:49:49.380 --> 00:49:51.360
And then speaking
of faith, for those

00:49:51.360 --> 00:49:53.413
who have been watching
these Academy dialogues,

00:49:53.413 --> 00:49:55.830
I really think they should
really be called game changers.

00:49:55.830 --> 00:49:58.170
Because every person on
here is a game changer.

00:49:58.170 --> 00:50:00.870
I just want to highlight, when
you look at Franklin Leonard.

00:50:00.870 --> 00:50:02.340
There was the black list.

00:50:02.340 --> 00:50:04.660
There was no one who had
done the black list before.

00:50:04.660 --> 00:50:08.220
Here he is as an executive
for Will and James Lassiter,

00:50:08.220 --> 00:50:10.800
and had this idea, why don't
I get some of my friends

00:50:10.800 --> 00:50:13.800
to rate the scripts
they've been reading?

00:50:13.800 --> 00:50:17.130
And out of that one idea
the industry changed.

00:50:17.130 --> 00:50:23.100
Debra Martin Chase came to
Hollywood from Harvard Law,

00:50:23.100 --> 00:50:27.870
has blazed a trail not only for
women, but for people of color

00:50:27.870 --> 00:50:30.750
in making the biggest
movies and now television

00:50:30.750 --> 00:50:32.588
shows the industry
has ever seen.

00:50:32.588 --> 00:50:35.130
But there was a moment, as she
mentioned, she wanted to quit.

00:50:35.130 --> 00:50:37.180
But she said I've
got to keep going.

00:50:37.180 --> 00:50:39.060
And as a result,
others can look to her

00:50:39.060 --> 00:50:40.970
and see that it's possible.

00:50:40.970 --> 00:50:46.350
Phil Sun, just
moments ago, was in,

00:50:46.350 --> 00:50:49.810
working for one of the
biggest agencies in Hollywood.

00:50:49.810 --> 00:50:52.200
But something in him
said, there's more.

00:50:52.200 --> 00:50:56.880
And he took the risk,
stepping out and starting M88.

00:50:56.880 --> 00:50:59.730
And now changing the game.

00:50:59.730 --> 00:51:03.270
If you are watching this,
the game can be changed.

00:51:03.270 --> 00:51:05.550
That is the takeaway.

00:51:05.550 --> 00:51:08.280
The system needs changing.

00:51:08.280 --> 00:51:09.870
It can be changed.

00:51:09.870 --> 00:51:15.230
And the only way to get it done
is for us to have the will.

00:51:15.230 --> 00:51:16.980
I've said it before
and I'll say it again.

00:51:16.980 --> 00:51:20.352
President John F Kennedy,
when he was in the space race,

00:51:20.352 --> 00:51:21.810
and he's trying to
get to the moon.

00:51:21.810 --> 00:51:23.650
How do we do it?

00:51:23.650 --> 00:51:27.070
His chief engineer
said, all we have

00:51:27.070 --> 00:51:30.820
to do is have the will to do it.

00:51:30.820 --> 00:51:33.720
I challenge you, if you're
listening to this conversation,

00:51:33.720 --> 00:51:37.530
have the will to make
change wherever you may be

00:51:37.530 --> 00:51:39.660
and wherever you may sit.

00:51:39.660 --> 00:51:41.550
You've been watching
Academy Dialogues.

00:51:41.550 --> 00:51:43.692
Thank you so much for
tuning in, and make

00:51:43.692 --> 00:51:45.150
sure you stay tuned
because there's

00:51:45.150 --> 00:51:49.018
a whole lot more of these
conversations coming to you.

